<p>A dramatic account of {% person "george-armstrong-custer" %}Custer{% endperson %}, the 7th Cavalry, life at {% place "fort-abraham-lincoln" %}Fort Abraham Lincoln{% endplace %} after the Civil War, and the Battle of the Little Bighorn was staged outdoors each summer at the {% place "custer-memorial-amphitheater" %}Custer Memorial Amphitheater{% endplace %} from 1959 through 1968. The original production, <em>Trail West</em>, was written by {% person "william-t-chichester" %}W. T. Chichester{% endperson %} and {% person "frederick-g-walsh" %}Frederick G. Walsh{% endperson %}. Walsh was instrumental in beginning operations at the Burning Hills Amphitheater in Medora with his drama <em>Old Four Eyes</em>, which highlighted the life of {% person "theodore-roosevelt" %}Theodore Roosevelt{% endperson %}. That production preceded the <em>Medora Musical</em>, which continues today. Mandan's Chamber of Commerce approached Walsh in 1958 to write a play for use at Fort Abraham Lincoln. The drama was staged beside the trail used by General Custer and his men when they left the fort on May 17, 1876.</p>

<p>In 1961, {% person "bert-pettey" %}Bert Pettey{% endperson %} completed a major rewrite of the original script. Beginning with the summer of 1962, the play was called <em>The Custer Drama</em>. Coauthor William Chichester directed the first season. {% person "george-poletes" %}George Poletes{% endperson %} directed from 1960 through 1962, {% person "joann-feldhusen-zoller" %}JoAnn (Feldhusen) Zoller{% endperson %} from 1963 through 1965, and {% person "winston-miller" %}Winston Miller{% endperson %} from 1966 through 1968. In 1961, the Centennial Players supplemented the regular offerings by staging <em>Dracula</em> at midnight after performances of <em>The Custer Drama</em>. People who showed a medical card listing AB-negative blood were admitted free.</p>

